WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.501
-It's time to play
a game of deception

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.504
called "True Confessions."

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:12.712
♪♪

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:13.979
Now, my first opponent tonight

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:16.616
is an American legend
and country music icon.

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:19.518
Please welcome Dolly Parton.

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:21.453
-Hi.
-Look at you.

00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:22.822
Oh, you are the best.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:25.491
Oh, Dolly, thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:28.394
Dolly and I will be facing off
against a very talented actor

00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:29.862
who stars in the new movie

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:31.898
"To All The Boys:
Always and Forever."

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:34.467
Give it up for Noah Centineo.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.302
-Oh! Ooh, wow.

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.504
You're already playing
the game, I see.

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:40.773
You got your game face.
-We're in it.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.208
-All right,
here's how this works.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:45.445
Each of us have two envelopes
containing confessions.

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.580
One is true, the other is a lie.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.115
Once you read your confession,

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.050
the other players will
interrogate you,

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.319
and they have to come to
a unanimous decision.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:55.587
Is it a truth, or is it a lie?

00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:57.023
Dolly, you will go first.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:58.957
Noah, which envelope
should she open,

00:00:58.957 --> 00:01:00.894
number one or number two?

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:01.894
-Hmm.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:03.429
Let's go number one.

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:04.564
-Yeah, I'm feeling that, too.
-This one?

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:05.799
-Yep, I'm feeling that, too.

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:06.799
-Okay.

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:08.133
-Here we go.
-All right.

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:09.602
Here I go.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.504
Oh, Lordy, I'm, like,
at the Academy Awards.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:14.106
Can't get it
out of the deal here.

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:16.442
Okay, sorry.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:21.814
-I dated a guy, and he kicked me
out of a car

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:24.684
and made me walk home at night
through a forest

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:27.520 line:0%
with bears and bobcats.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.488 line:0%
-[ Laughing ] What?

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:30.623 line:0%
All right. Wait a second.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:32.559 line:0%
All right.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:33.659 line:0%
-What year?

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:35.394 line:0%
Do you remember --
What year was this?

00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:36.729 line:0%
-Oh, I was a teenager.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.397 line:0%
I was about --

00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:41.601 line:0%
Oh, I was 17, 18 years old.
-Okay, 17.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.970 line:0%
-Did you encounter bears
and bobcats,

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.905 line:0%
or were they just present?
-Absolutely.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:48.240 line:0%
-Good question.
-I heard them, felt them.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.043 line:0%
-Do you remember
what car it was?

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:53.579 line:0%
-Well, I know it was
a really good car,

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:56.049 line:0%
'cause I had a date with a boy,

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.718 line:0%
and I only dated him
because he had a car.

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:02.789 line:0%
-[ Laughs ]
-And so it was a nice car.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.624 line:0%
I think it was a Chevy,
maybe a '57.

00:02:05.624 --> 00:02:06.826 line:0%
Something like that.

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:08.828 line:0%
-Why did he kick you
out of the car?

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.897 line:0%
What was the thing
that happened?

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:15.368 line:0%
-Well, it was a "put out
or get out," uh, situation.

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:17.137 line:0%
-Ah. You stepped.
-So he kicked me out,

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:18.837 line:0%
told me to walk home.
-And you stepped.

00:02:18.837 --> 00:02:20.106 line:0%
I like that.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.274 line:0%
-Put out or get out.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:25.410 line:0%
-Where was this forest?

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.579 line:0%
-Well, this was up in

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.281 line:0%
The Smoky Mountains
of East Tennessee,

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:30.182 line:0%
and we were about five miles
from my home,

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:33.186 line:0%
and that's way back
in the mountains,

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.755 line:0%
I mean, where there are bears
and bobcats.

00:02:35.755 --> 00:02:38.024 line:0%
And there are those toothless

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.860 line:0%
"Deliverance"
kind of mountain men,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.429 line:0%
in addition to
the bears and all that.

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:44.630 line:0%
-Yeah, the bears
don't even have teeth.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.667 line:0%
-That was not
a pleasant place to be.

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.169 line:0%
-Noah, I don't know.

00:02:50.169 --> 00:02:51.704 line:0%
Dolly is also --

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:55.241 line:0%
Not only is she a talented
singer and songwriter,

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.243 line:0%
but she's also
a very good actor.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:59.245 line:0%
So I don't know if this is --

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:02.515 line:0%
she could be playing us
like a fiddle.

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:04.783 line:0%
-I'm feeling like
I'm being played,

00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:06.052 line:0%
to be honest with you.

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:09.255 line:0%
-But I also feel like Dolly
is very strong,

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.889 line:0%
and she would do something
like that.

00:03:10.889 --> 00:03:13.225 line:0%
Like, yeah.
-Oh, absolutely.

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:16.662 line:0%
I think it's -- honest,
I think she's telling the truth.

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:20.133 line:0%
-Gosh. I'm going to say, yes,
it's true.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:21.634
-It is true!

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:22.935
-[ Laughing ] Oh, my gosh.

00:03:22.935 --> 00:03:24.269
-It is true!

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:25.872
-No way.
-I swear it's true.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.174
But I did learn something
that night, though.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.776
I thought, if that ever happens
to me again,

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:31.444
I'm definitely giving it up.

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:34.514
[ Laughter ]
I would rather be --

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:37.717
I would rather be
chewed on by a boy

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:39.051
than a bear.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:42.088
And so I thought that --
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:43.956
You are the best.

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:45.190
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.625
-Okay. It is my turn.

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:49.796
Which envelope should I open,
number one or number two?

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:51.363
Dolly?
-Dolly, go ahead.

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:52.966
-I think number two.

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:54.900
-Okay.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.970
Do do do do-doo. Okay.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.106
I was at a party
at someone's house,

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:04.544
and I heard Jeff Bezos say,
"Alexa, who's your daddy?"

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:06.579 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:11.551 line:0%
-Do you remember
what Alexa said in return?

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:14.753 line:0%
-Uh, I don't know
if she answered.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:16.789 line:0%
I just thought
it was just bizarre

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:19.892 line:0%
that I could not --
I can't believe Jeff Bezos,

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.160 line:0%
why would he --

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.395 line:0%
that he would just lean in --

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:23.963 line:0%
He didn't even know I was there,
I don't think.

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:26.365 line:0%
He just kind of leaned in
and just said it to Alexa.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.134 line:0%
And I was like,
"Did I just see that?"

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:29.802 line:0%
-So, wait. Did he whisper it?

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:33.639 line:0%
It was like a -- It was like a
gentle, like, whisper to Alexa?

00:04:33.639 --> 00:04:35.174 line:0%
-No he didn't whisper
"Who's your daddy?"

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.477 line:0%
But he just kinda --
No, he said out loud.

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:40.346 line:0%
I think he -- I think he knew...

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:44.250 line:0%
Yeah, he knew that he was
being funny, probably.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.486 line:0%
-Do you use Alexa a lot?

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:48.954 line:0%
Are you one of those people
that really do all that,

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:51.524 line:0%
ask all these crazy things
and ask for jokes

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:53.059 line:0%
and all these weird people?

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:55.361 line:0%
-I do. I use it all the time.
I talk to --

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:58.430 line:0%
But this is just weird,
random at a party,

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.634 line:0%
and I just saw it happen,
and it's almost --

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.069 line:0%
It's, like, rare that you
would ever see

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:06.839 line:0%
the guy who invented Alexa
say to Alexa,

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:09.341 line:0%
"Who's your daddy?"

00:05:09.341 --> 00:05:10.876 line:0%
-And what was he wearing?

00:05:10.876 --> 00:05:13.178 line:0%
What was -- What was Bezos
wearing?

00:05:13.178 --> 00:05:15.781 line:0%
-Wearing almost
this exact same outfit.

00:05:15.781 --> 00:05:18.050 line:0%
I am dressed like Jeff Bezos
from that --

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.552 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:24.390 line:0%
I'm wearing the Jeff Bezos line
of suits. Yeah.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.293 line:0%
-Well, it is time for us
to say that's a lie?

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.028 line:0%
-A lie, too.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:31.630 line:0%
-[ Laughs ]
I think that's a lie.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.332 line:0%
-It is the truth.

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.234
-Is it really?
-What?

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:36.268
-Oh, okay.

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:38.303
-And I was like, "What?!"

00:05:38.303 --> 00:05:40.839
That is something
I will never ever forget, ever.

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:42.241
It is a true story.
-I guess not.

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:45.111
Well, that's a good one.
I thought that was a lie.

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:46.445
-Okay.
-That's incredible.

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:48.815
-We've got two truths now.

00:05:48.815 --> 00:05:49.915
-Yeah, here we go.
-Let's see what --

00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:51.083
-Oh, interesting.
You're right.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:53.419
Noah, now it is your turn.

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:54.686
Dolly, which envelope
should he open,

00:05:54.686 --> 00:05:57.356
number one or number two?

00:05:57.356 --> 00:05:58.857
-I think number one.
-Yup, number one.

00:05:58.857 --> 00:05:59.892
-All right.
-Yeah.

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:02.294
In honor of all Dolly's songs.

00:06:02.294 --> 00:06:03.830
Number one.
-Okay.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:04.930
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:07.499
-I want to make this
a little dramatic.

00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:09.668
-[ Chuckling ] Oh, my goodness.

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:11.003
Wow.

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:12.772
-Dun, dun, dun, dun, dun.

00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:15.374
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.374 --> 00:06:18.444
-Oh, that's really --
-I once locked my father

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:21.914 line:0%
in a meat locker for two hours
before telling someone.

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:24.750 line:0%
-Why did you wait so long
if you accidentally did it?

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:28.754 line:0%
-I was 9 years old,
and I-I panicked.

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:30.155 line:0%
And, like,
I tried to get him out,

00:06:30.155 --> 00:06:33.225 line:0%
and then we were on a tour,
and so --

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:35.127 line:0%
-A tour of a meat locker?

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:36.829 line:0%
-Well, no,
it was a meat facility.

00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:41.400 line:0%
He owned, like,
a restaurant back then.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.735 line:0%
And so we were looking

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.469 line:0%
at different, like, meats
and stuff.

00:06:44.469 --> 00:06:45.971 line:0%
And he took me on it,

00:06:45.971 --> 00:06:48.241 line:0%
but it was with a group
of, like, all of his members.

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:49.508 line:0%
And so --

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:50.809 line:0%
-Were you the only
9-year-old there,

00:06:50.809 --> 00:06:52.878 line:0%
or was it a bunch of kids
going around a meat locker?

00:06:52.878 --> 00:06:55.348 line:0%
-No, no, I was the only one.
I was the only one.

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:57.349 line:0%
-Did you get along
with your father?

00:06:57.349 --> 00:06:58.917 line:0%
Did you like him?
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:06:58.917 --> 00:07:00.119 line:0%
-Oh, okay.

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:01.387 line:0%
I just wanted to make sure

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.656 line:0%
that you weren't trying to
really get rid of him.

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:05.324 line:0%
-Was some part of you
kind of excited

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:06.558 line:0%
that you did lock your dad --

00:07:06.558 --> 00:07:07.793 line:0%
How did you know
that he was locked in there?

00:07:07.793 --> 00:07:09.795 line:0%
-That's what I --
[ Laughs ]

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:12.330 line:0%
-He went in there,
and I closed the door.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:15.367 line:0%
And then I realized that it
locks from the outside,

00:07:15.367 --> 00:07:17.303 line:0%
but I didn't know
how to unlock it.

00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:20.305 line:0%
And I was embarrassed to,
like, go and tell someone

00:07:20.305 --> 00:07:21.641 line:0%
that I had locked him in there.

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.809 line:0%
-How long was your dad
in the meat locker?

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:25.010 line:0%
-[ Laughing ] For two hours.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.913 line:0%
-What?! No, no.

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:29.849 line:0%
-Was he, like, frozen?

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:32.185 line:0%
Did it, like,
make him sick or what?

00:07:32.185 --> 00:07:34.386 line:0%
-No, they're not that cold.
Like --

00:07:34.386 --> 00:07:35.688 line:0%
-That's a good story.

00:07:35.688 --> 00:07:38.991 line:0%
Whether it's a truth or a lie,
that's an amazing story.

00:07:38.991 --> 00:07:40.726 line:0%
-Oh, no.
-It's scary. Wow.

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:42.161 line:0%
-I'm baffled, Dolly.
What do you think?

00:07:42.161 --> 00:07:44.630 line:0%
I'm torn, because --
-I think --

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.266 line:0%
I kind of think
that might be true.

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:48.934 line:0%
-I think it's a lie.

00:07:48.934 --> 00:07:50.068 line:0%
I think he's playing us.

00:07:50.068 --> 00:07:52.571 line:0%
I think he did
the sheet over his head.

00:07:52.571 --> 00:07:53.873
He made the story up.

00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:55.241
And he's an actor.

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:56.809
I'm going to say it's a lie.

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:58.344
-It's a lie. Yeah, it's a lie.

00:07:58.344 --> 00:07:59.979
-Yes! Oh, my God.

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:01.547
[ Laughter ]

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:02.948
-Well, that was
a good one, though.

00:08:02.948 --> 00:08:05.418
-That was not bad, dude.

00:08:05.418 --> 00:08:06.885
We got a true confession.
-That was very good.

00:08:06.885 --> 00:08:09.355
-My thanks to Dolly Parton
and Noah Centineo.

